    "description": "Background The Ethiopian Center for Disability and Development (ECDD) is a non-governmental, non-partisan, and non-profit organization committed to promoting the rights, inclusion, and well-being of persons with disabilities in Ethiopia. ECDD works to advance disability inclusion through policy advocacy, capacity building, service delivery, and partnerships with governmental and non-governmental actors. Under the Forced Begging Among Persons with Disabilities Project, implemented in collaboration with Population Council and ELDA, ECDD supports children with disabilities and other survivors rescued from forced begging, trafficking, and exploitation in Addis Ababa. These children are accommodated in partner shelters where they receive education, rehabilitation, assistive devices, psychosocial support, and reintegration services. Many of the children have experienced severe trauma, neglect, abuse, and exploitation and therefore require specialized and disability-inclusive psychosocial care. To strengthen the provision of psychosocial support services, ECDD seeks to recruit two qualified Counsellors (one female and one male) who will provide accessible, trauma-informed, and child-friendly counselling services for project participants residing in partner shelters across Addis Ababa. Purpose of the Role The Counsellors will be responsible for providing professional counselling and psychosocial support services to children with disabilities and other survivors rescued from forced begging, trafficking, and related forms of exploitation. The position aims to support the emotional recovery, psychological wellbeing, resilience, and social reintegration of beneficiaries through individualized, disability-inclusive, and trauma-informed interventions. The Counsellors will also provide technical support to caregivers and shelter personnel to ensure that children receive appropriate psychosocial care and protection. Key Responsibilities A. Counselling and Psychosocial Support Services Provide individual and group counselling sessions to children with disabilities and other rescued survivors residing in partner shelters. Conduct psychosocial and mental health assessments for newly admitted children and develop appropriate support plans. Monitor and document the psychological progress and wellbeing of beneficiaries. Utilize trauma-informed, child-friendly, play-based, and creative counselling approaches tailored to the age, developmental stage, and disability status of each child. Ensure counselling services are accessible for children with physical, sensory, intellectual, communication, and multiple disabilities. Provide crisis response and immediate psychosocial interventions when required. Identify and refer beneficiaries requiring specialized psychiatric or medical treatment to appropriate service providers. Promote emotional resilience, self-confidence, coping mechanisms, and social reintegration among beneficiaries. B. Capacity Building and Support to Caregivers Provide technical guidance and coaching to shelter staff and caregivers on disability-inclusive and trauma-informed care practices. Facilitate awareness and capacity-building sessions for shelter personnel on child protection, psychosocial wellbeing, communication techniques, and behavioural management. Support partner shelters to create safe, nurturing, accessible, and child-friendly environments that enhance the wellbeing of children. Contribute to multidisciplinary case management processes and collaborate with other service providers supporting beneficiaries. C. Protection, Safeguarding, and Inclusion Ensure that all counselling services adhere to child safeguarding principles and ethical standards. Maintain strict confidentiality and protection of beneficiary information and records. Identify and promptly report safeguarding concerns, protection risks, abuse, neglect, exploitation, or other incidents in accordance with ECDD safeguarding policies and procedures. Promote disability inclusion, gender sensitivity, and the rights of children and persons with disabilities throughout all interventions. Respect and uphold the principles of dignity, participation, non-discrimination, and informed consent in all interactions with beneficiaries. D. Monitoring, Documentation, and Reporting Maintain accurate, confidential, and up-to-date case files and counselling records. Prepare and submit weekly and monthly reports detailing counselling services provided, client progress, challenges encountered, and referrals made. Collect and maintain service data disaggregated by age, sex, and disability. Contribute to project monitoring, evaluation, learning, and reporting activities. Document success stories, lessons learned, and emerging psychosocial needs to support project improvement and reporting requirements. E. Coordination and Collaboration Coordinate closely with ECDD project staff, shelter management, caregivers, health professionals, and external service providers. Participate in case conferences, review meetings, and project learning events with donor and partners Establish and maintain referral pathways for specialized mental health, protection, rehabilitation, and social services. Represent ECDD in relevant psychosocial support and child protection coordination platforms when required.",
