    "datePosted": "2026-11-26T07:00:32.000Z",
    "description": "1. Job Purpose The Finance Manager is responsible for leading and managing the Company's financial operations for its import, export and local trading activities. The position ensures sound financial planning, accurate accounting and reporting, effective cash and working-capital management, statutory and regulatory compliance, and strong internal controls. The role provides timely financial information and practical advice to management to support profitable growth, liquidity, risk management and informed business decisions. 2. Key Duties and Responsibilities A. Financial Management, Planning and Reporting Lead the preparation of annual budgets, periodic forecasts, cash-flow projections and financial plans in line with business objectives. Prepare and present accurate monthly, quarterly and annual financial statements and management reports, including variance and profitability analysis. Monitor revenue, gross margins, operating costs, expenses and financial performance by product, transaction, business line and customer where applicable. Provide management with financial analysis, recommendations and early warnings on financial risks and performance gaps. B. Import, Export and Foreign-Currency Transactions Oversee financial aspects of import and export transactions, including supplier payments, customer receipts, foreign-currency requirements and related bank documentation. Coordinate with banks and relevant internal teams on letters of credit (LCs), documentary collections, transfers, guarantees and other trade-finance arrangements, as applicable. Monitor foreign-exchange exposure, exchange-rate differences, foreign-currency commitments and the proper recording and reconciliation of related transactions. Ensure import costs are properly accumulated and allocated, including freight, insurance, customs duties, taxes, clearing, inland transport and other landed costs. Monitor export proceeds, receivables and supporting documentation, and ensure transactions are recorded and followed up in accordance with applicable requirements. C. Accounting, Tax and Regulatory Compliance Ensure timely and accurate recording of all financial transactions and maintenance of complete books of accounts and supporting documents. Oversee accounts payable, accounts receivable, general ledger, bank reconciliations, fixed assets, inventory accounting and period-end closing. Ensure compliance with applicable Ethiopian tax laws and reporting obligations, including VAT, withholding tax, income tax and other relevant statutory requirements. Coordinate statutory audits, tax audits and other external reviews; ensure audit findings are addressed within agreed timelines. Maintain up-to-date knowledge of relevant financial, tax, customs and foreign-exchange rules affecting the Company, and advise management on their implications. D. Cash, Credit and Working-Capital Management Manage daily cash position, liquidity, banking relationships and payment priorities to ensure availability of funds for business operations. Develop and monitor policies and procedures for customer credit, collection, supplier payments and credit limits. Monitor receivable and payable ageing, follow up overdue balances and recommend appropriate actions to reduce collection delays and financing costs. Optimize working capital through effective management of cash, inventory, receivables and payables. E. Costing, Inventory and Trading Operations Establish and maintain appropriate product and transaction costing methods for imported goods and locally traded products. Work closely with Procurement, Sales, Warehouse and Operations teams to reconcile purchases, sales, stock movements, inventory balances and cost of goods sold. Review inventory valuation, slow-moving and obsolete stock, stock adjustments and gross-profit margins, and report material variances. Support pricing decisions by providing reliable cost, margin, break-even and profitability analysis. F. Internal Control, Risk and Team Leadership Establish, implement and continuously improve financial policies, procedures, approval limits and internal control systems. Safeguard Company assets and minimize risks relating to fraud, errors, unauthorized payments, credit exposure and financial misstatement. Lead, coach and evaluate Finance Department employees; allocate responsibilities and build staff capability and accountability. Promote effective use of accounting/ERP systems, ensure data integrity and maintain appropriate financial records and document controls. Perform other related duties assigned by the immediate supervisor.",
